# symrules.rows -- GENERATED by `gates/symrules-census.py --rows`, which is its ONLY writer.
#
# WHAT THIS IS. One row per UPSTREAM rewrite rule, in UPSTREAM'S OWN FIRST-WINS ORDER,
# with the OP SET THE RULE FIRES ON (upstream's own `pdict` key set, read off the live
# matcher) and whether the port has it. The order is the COMPOSITION order of
# `tinygrad/uop/symbolic.py` -- `sym = symbolic + pm_simplify_valid + ... +
# pm_clean_up_group_sink` -- and NOT a line order, because the order is what decides which
# of two matching rewrites wins. `pm_remove_invalid` is a SEPARATE matcher and is never
# composed into `sym`, so its two rules are the LAST two rows and the port's `rm_table`
# is matched against them.
#
# WHAT MAKES IT STABLE. A REVISION, not a timestamp: the UPSTREAM PIN and the git blob id
# of every file this census read. Those are content-addressed, so a change to any of
# them makes the header stop matching and `gates/symrules-gate.py` says so. A table that
# recorded a DATE would be true of the tree on that date and of no other.
#
# HOW A PORT ENTRY IS MATCHED TO AN UPSTREAM RULE. By the upstream line the port's own
# table cites, and then CHECKED: the port's op set must be a SUBSET of the upstream rule's
# op set. A stale citation fails that test, because the line it names is a DIFFERENT
# rule with a DIFFERENT op set. `gates/symrules-gate.py` REFUSES on a mapping that does
# not hold; it does not print a table it cannot vouch for.
#
# COLUMNS: n | file:line | ops (the op set it fires on) | port table | port tag | state
#   state: PORTED  the port has it, and the op set is EQUAL to upstream's
#          NARROW  the port has it and the mapping holds, but the port's op set is a
#                  STRICT SUBSET of upstream's: the port fires on FEWER ops, so it
#                  SKIPS nodes upstream rewrites. A divergence, not a break.
#          MISSING the port has no entry for it
#          BROKEN  the port has an entry and the subset test FAILED
#
